\pard\keep\keepn\widctlpar\s1\sb360\sa80\sl252\slmult1\cf1\kerning2\i\f0\fs40 The Great Gatsby\i0 : Nick Carraway as Narrator\par

\pard\keep\keepn\widctlpar\s2\sb160\sa80\sl252\slmult1\fs32 Prof Michael Nowlin\par

\pard\widctlpar\cf0\f1\fs24 University of Victoria\par
\par
Now in Max Perkins's letter to Fitzgerald after reading that typescript of \i Gatsby\i0 , the first thing he singled out for praise was Fitzgerald's narrative method. And this is what he wrote:\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 You adopted exactly the right method of telling it, that of employing a narrator who is more of a spectator than an actor; this puts the reader upon a point of observation on a higher level than that on which the characters stand and at a distance that gives perspective. In no other way could your irony have been so immensely effective.\par

\pard\widctlpar\par

\pard\widctlpar\qr Maxwell Perkins, letter to F. Scott Fitzgerald, 20 November, 1924\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
It's been a long-standing question in \i Gatsby\i0  criticism about whether Nick Caraway is a reliable narrator or not. Fitzgerald's first critical reader clearly thought him the best witness we could have of the characters and the actions in this story and Fitzgerald never said anything to contradict that. Nick does indeed function more as a spectator than an actor, though he does bring his cousin Daisy, together with Gatsby. But in what sense is he perched on a higher level than that on which the other characters stand? What's the source of this distance that gives perspective? For, surely, Nick's is no god-like perspective. He's a very human character with prejudices, memories, habits, yearnings, and inhibitions. So, he's actually the most complex character in the novel. He's actually fairly well-rounded. He tells us a good deal about himself, at key points in the novel, not least the introductory pages. It is revealing these qualities in him that I think make him Gatsby's ultimate confidant and Gatsby's ultimate champion. But on the face of things, he also establishes himself as Gatsby's opposite. He remembers and abides by the precepts of his father and in this novel, which is centered on the transformation of American mores unleashed, you know, in the aftermath of the First World War, set in the summer of 1922, which Fitzgerald would later see as the summer that really began The Jazz Age (as he called it), Nick will come across as sounding like the last bearer of good manners and traditional values, to some extent, at least comparatively. But Nick's values are clearly class based:\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 My family have been prominent, well-to-do people in this Middle Western city for three generations. (8)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
So hence, the fatherly advice he remembers:\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\ldblquote Whenever you feel like criticizing anyone\rdblquote , he told me, just remember that all the people in this world haven't had the advantages that you've had.\rdblquote  (7)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
And so, Nick's learned practice of reserving judgments made him a natural confidant when he was at college. Of course, he went to an Ivy League College: Yale. \highlight2 He goes on to describe the Carraways, his family, as something of a clan who've actually invented a tradition that they're descended from the Dukes of Buccleuch \endash  as though they're aristocrats. In truth, their money comes from the wholesale hardware business that his immigrant great uncle founde\highlight0 d.\par
\par
And as for \i noblesse oblige\i0 , the fact that the great uncle ended up buying a substitute, so he didn't have to serve in the Civil War (8). Nick can cast an ironic light on his family\rquote s somewhat pretentious traditions \endash  and I think this reflects a degree of self-awareness that's one of his qualities as a narrator. But I think it also reflects how comfortable he is with that, how he identifies with that family, and particularly how he identifies with his father, his father's values. Gatsby is someone who will simply tell people his father's dead. He's the opposite in that regard. He denies his father.\par
\par
Now, if we're tempted also to think of Nick as a bit of a snob, again, he anticipates us and diffuses our judgment with this self-deprecating irony and he says: \par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 as my father snobbishly suggested, and I snobbishly repeat, a sense of the fundamental decencies is parcelled out unequally at birth. (7) \par

\pard\widctlpar\par
So again, as Gatsby's antithesis in some ways and as bearer of the fundamental decencies we might expect Nick to depict Gatsby ironically, and I think, of course, he does depict him ironically at certain points in the novel. He tells us on the first page the novel that Gatsby, the man who gives his name to the book:\par
\par
Represented everything for which I have an unaffected scorn. (8) \par
\par
And I like that \ldblquote unaffected scorn.\rdblquote  It is important. Gatsby is as we'll discover a flamboyant liar, a bootlegger, a dealer in crooked stocks and bonds, and the lover of a married woman. But presumably his more general offense is that he's a shameless social climber. Yet Nick is telling the story to honor Gatsby, to single him out and redeem him from what preyed on him as he says, \ldblquote what foul dust floated in the wake of his dreams\rdblquote . (8) Nick is writing this story in reaction to what he witnessed and participated in during that summer of 1922, after having left the east, and returned to his father's home. So what has come to make him see Gatsby in the way that he does, so that we might come to see Gatsby as \ldblquote great\rdblquote ? Or, more importantly, to see Gatsby's \i tragedy\i0 , as a measure of the moral vacuity of a plutocratic American society that Nick is a relatively privileged member of.\par
\par
Nick's harsh judgment of the likes of his wealthier cousin, Daisy, and on Tom Buchanan, I think, surely rings sincerely. So, he's a he's a reliable narrator, I think, in far as the judgments he ultimately makes really come out of the experience that he undergoes as an observant, receptive participant in the events ensuing upon his arrival in West Egg, after having come east to start a career as a bond salesman in Manhattan. He often comes across as a neutral, dead-pan recorder of facts and events \endash  sometimes very comically so, as in that brilliant description of Gatsby's guests at the beginning of chapter 4. But at key moments, his perspective becomes romantic and really visionary, I think, and this is where Fitzgerald moves from prose to poetry. It becomes very, very poetic in these passages.\par
\par
Nick's visionary inclinations are awakened at one point early at Gatsby's party thanks to two finger bowls of champagne. And as he feels the alcohol, he says:\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 the scene had changed before my eyes into something significant, elemental, and profound. (48)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
The novel depicts a society of very entrancing surfaces and, in telling the story, Nick ultimately gives us the significant elemental, profound meaning underlying this chaos of superficiality that he also so aptly registers. He picks it up and describes it perfectly. And thus his perspective is finally an evaluative one. Gatsby is great. The society that failed him is rotten. And what makes Gatsby great seems finally this capacity for belief. Gatsby believed in \ldblquote the green light.\rdblquote  And this vitality he brings to the project of acting on this belief so that he can make his dreams about himself come true.\par
\par
What gives the novel its note of sustained irony is that Nick writes from the standpoint of disillusionment, about a peculiarly American hero driven by illusions. And Fitzgerald said to a friend soon after the novel was published that the novel's about lost illusions.\par
\par
We must finally factor into our understanding of this novel some ideas about the sources of Nick's disillusionment. The easiest answer would be that he's a war veteran. Fitzgerald did not see action overseas. I don't think he convincingly depicts him as [a veteran]. It\rquote s just kind of standard to make a character a war veteran in, in this period, obviously, because of what happened. But I think Nick seems more traumatized by the events of 1922 than anything he saw in 1918.\par
\par
Another odd detail, in heading to New York from the Midwest, to learn the bond business in 1922. Nick's nearly thirty years old. He turns \i thirty\i0  in the novel, and yet he's treated almost like a 16-year-old by his family. He tells us they were all they were they were choosing a career for him like \ldblquote they were choosing a prep school\rdblquote  (9) and his father is funding him for a year. When he settles in West Egg and confidently gives a newcomer directions, he feels \ldblquote lonely no longer\rdblquote  and he half ironically thinks of himself as \ldblquote a pathfinder, an original settler\rdblquote  (9) as though this eastward journey, which, of course, reverses the westward journey of the pioneers, connects him somehow to these original Americans. But I think we might read something into that reference to loneliness which squares with other details in Nick's self-portrait. He has difficulty sustaining amorous relationships with women. We learn at the end of the chapter 3 that he spends a lot of nights wandering the streets of Manhattan and he says:\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 At the enchanted metropolitan twilight I felt a haunting loneliness sometimes, and I felt it in others. (57)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
In the midst of the party at Myrtle Wilson's apartment in chapter 2, he identifies with this solitary walker, outside in the streets, looking up at the lights, the lit-up apartment where the party's happening and he thinks he imagined a connection to that that person. He says:\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 I was him too, looking up and wondering. I was within and without, simultaneously enchanted and repelled by the inexhaustible variety of life. (37) \par

\pard\widctlpar\par
And then there's that rather cryptic response at the end of that verbal description of Gatsby's first kiss with Daisy. And Nick says: \par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 Through all he said, even through his appalling sentimentality, I was reminded of something \emdash an elusive rhythm, a fragment of lost words, that I had heard somewhere a long time ago. (107)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
There are some implausible details, I think, that attach to Nick's character. And not the least of which is the idea that he actually is writing this novel. I would suggest finally that it's because he is a lonely, solitary, detached artist figure and yet he's inhabiting the character of a relatively conservative bond salesman in Manhattan \endash  an emotionally inhibited bond salesman too. I think Fitzgerald's \i tour de force\i0  was to disguise this problem well through otherwise consistent characterization of Nick, and the compelling force of Nick's prose and the rightness of his judgments.\par
\par
And so, as the voice of the writer, in a way, I think, he's  as authoritative a first person narrator as we're going to get.\par

